<%# The body of the message only — emails/layout.html.erb wraps it. Colours are
    inline as well as in the layout's stylesheet, for the clients that drop a
    <style> block.

    Two messages share this template. With a club it's an invitation: somebody
    already set up has been added to a club, so it has to say what that club is
    and what the site does before it asks for a click. Without one it's the
    /login form's own sign-in link, which the reader asked for seconds ago and
    wants nothing but the link. %>
<% if club -%>
<p class="first" style="color:#d8e0e8">You've been added to <strong><%= club.name %></strong> on Boxd Club — a small private movie club that picks what its members watch next by vote.</p>
<p style="color:#d8e0e8"><%= club.mode_invite_note %> You rank it, there's no deadline, and the round closes once everyone has voted. Then you all watch the winner and log it on Letterboxd, and the next round opens.</p>
<p style="color:#d8e0e8">You already have an account, so there's nothing to set up:</p>
<%= Mailer.button(login_url, "Open #{club.name}") %>
<% else -%>
<p class="first" style="color:#d8e0e8">Here's your way in. No password, just this link:</p>
<%= Mailer.button(login_url, "Sign in") %>
<% end -%>
<p class="meta last" style="color:#8b9aa8;font-size:14px">It keeps working for a couple of months, so you can open it again later. If you didn't ask for it, ignore it — it only signs in <%= user.email %>.</p>
